  "cached_tex": "\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{amsthm}\n\\usepackage{fontspec}\n\\usepackage{geometry}\n\\geometry{a4paper, margin=1in}\n\n\\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\n\\newtheorem{proposition}{Proposition}\n\\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}\n\\newtheorem{corollary}{Corollary}\n\\theoremstyle{definition}\n\\newtheorem{definition}{Definition}\n\\newtheorem{remark}{Remark}\n\n\\title{Mathematical Solution and Verification Report}\n\\author{}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\section*{Problem Statement}\n\n\\subsection*{The setting}\n\nWe work over $\\mathbb{C}$ here. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional vector space over $\\mathbb{C}$ of dimension $n+1$ with a direct sum decomposition\n$$V = W \\oplus L, \\quad L = \\langle e_{n+1} \\rangle$$\nwhere $\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_{n+1}\\}$ is a basis of $V$, $W = \\langle e_1, \\ldots, e_n \\rangle$, and $e_{n+1}$ spans the 1-dimensional complement. Let $G_1 = \\mathrm{GL}(W) = \\mathrm{GL}_n$ and $G_2 = \\mathrm{GL}(V) = \\mathrm{GL}_{n+1}$. We view $G_1$ as a subgroup of $G_2$ via the block embedding $g \\mapsto \\mathrm{diag}(g, 1)$ (so $G_1$ stabilizes $W$ and fixes $e_{n+1}$). Let $\\mathfrak{g}_1 = \\mathfrak{gl}_n$ and $\\mathfrak{g}_2 = \\mathfrak{gl}_{n+1}$ be the Lie algebras. Then we have a natural inclusion $\\mathfrak{g}_1 \\subset \\mathfrak{g}_2$.\n\nLet $G = G_1 \\times G_2 = \\mathrm{GL}_n \\times \\mathrm{GL}_{n+1}$ and $X = G_2 = \\mathrm{GL}_{n+1}$. $X$ is a $G$-spherical variety via the action\n$$(g_1, g_2) \\cdot g = g_1 g g_2^{-1}, \\quad (g_1, g_2) \\in G_1 \\times G_2,\\; g \\in \\mathrm{GL}_{n+1}.$$\n\nLet $M = T^*(X)$ be the cotangent bundle of $X$. Then $M$ is a Hamiltonian $G$-variety with the moment map $\\mu = \\mu_1 \\times \\mu_2 \\colon M \\to \\mathfrak{g}_1^* \\times \\mathfrak{g}_2^*$.\nWrite $M = \\mathrm{GL}_{n+1} \\times \\mathfrak{g}_2^*$. Let $p \\colon \\mathfrak{g}_2^* \\to \\mathfrak{g}_1^*$ be the natural projection. Then the moment map is given by\n$$\\mu_1(g, \\xi) = p(\\mathrm{Ad}^*(g)\\xi), \\quad \\mu_2(g, \\xi) = -\\xi, \\quad g \\in G_2,\\; \\xi \\in \\mathfrak{g}_2^*.$$\nWe identify $\\mathfrak{g}_1^*$ and $\\mathfrak{g}_2^*$ with $\\mathfrak{g}_1$ and $\\mathfrak{g}_2$ via the trace form.\n\nWe denote by $\\mathcal{P}_n$ the set of all partitions of $n$. The nilpotent orbits of $\\mathfrak{g}_1 = \\mathfrak{gl}_n$ and $\\mathfrak{g}_2 = \\mathfrak{gl}_{n+1}$ are parameterized by $\\mathcal{P}_n$ and $\\mathcal{P}_{n+1}$ respectively. We denote by $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$ the nilpotent orbit corresponding to a partition $\\lambda$.\n\nWe are interested in the following question:\n\n\\textbf{Question}\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item For which pairs of partitions $(\\lambda, \\mu) \\in \\mathcal{P}_n \\times \\mathcal{P}_{n+1}$ does the nilpotent orbit $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mu(M)$? By (1), this is equivalent to asking: for which pairs of partitions $(\\lambda, \\mu) \\in \\mathcal{P}_n \\times \\mathcal{P}_{n+1}$ does there exist $g \\in G_2$ and $\\xi \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ such that $\\mathrm{Ad}(g)\\xi|_W \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$?\n    \\item Let $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subseteq \\mathfrak{g}_1$ and $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mathfrak{g}_2$ be nilpotent orbits such that $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mu(M)$. We choose a point $e = (e_\\lambda, e_\\mu) \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ and consider the fiber $M_e = \\mu^{-1}(e)$. Find a closed formula for $\\dim M_e$.\n    \\item We know that\n    $$\\dim M_e \\leq \\frac{1}{2} \\dim M - \\frac{1}{2} (\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu).$$\n    We call $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ a \\emph{relevant} orbit if the above inequality is an equality. Find all the relevant nilpotent orbits.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\section*{Solution}\n\n\\subsection*{1. Summary}\n\n\\subsubsection*{a. Verdict}\n\nI have found a complete solution for the cases where $\\lambda=(1^n)$ (the zero orbit), $\\lambda=(n)$ (the regular nilpotent orbit), and for the specific dimension $n=3$. The reasoning and calculations in the previous version contained several errors which have now been corrected.\n\nThe relevant pairs $(\\lambda, \\mu)$ in the fully analyzed cases are:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Case $\\lambda=(1^n)$:}\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item $\\mu=(1^{n+1})$ (Zero-Zero)\n        \\item $\\mu=(2, 1^{n-1})$ (Zero-Minimal)\n        \\item $\\mu=(3, 1^{n-2})$ for $n \\ge 2$ (Zero-Subminimal)\n    \\end{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Case $\\lambda=(n)$:}\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item $\\mu=(n+1)$ (Regular-Regular)\n        \\item $\\mu=(n,1)$ (Regular-Subregular)\n    \\end{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Complete classification for $n=3$:}\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item If $\\lambda=(1^3)$: $\\mu \\in \\{(1^4), (2,1^2), (3,1)\\}$.\n        \\item If $\\lambda=(2,1)$: $\\mu \\in \\{(3,1), (2,2), (2,1,1)\\}$.\n        \\item If $\\lambda=(3)$: $\\mu \\in \\{(4), (3,1)\\}$.\n    \\end{itemize}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection*{b. Method Sketch}\n\n\\textbf{Part 1:} The condition $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mu(M)$ is equivalent to the existence of a matrix $Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ whose top-left $n \\times n$ principal submatrix, denoted $\\pi(Y)$, lies in $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$. This holds if and only if the partition $\\lambda$ is obtained from $\\mu$ by decreasing one part by 1 (denoted $\\lambda \\prec \\mu$).\n\n\\textbf{Part 2:} The dimension of the fiber $M_e = \\mu^{-1}(e)$ for $e=(e_\\lambda, e_\\mu)$ is computed. The fiber is isomorphic to the variety $\\{g \\in \\mathrm{GL}_{n+1} \\mid \\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)e_\\mu) = -e_\\lambda\\}$. The dimension of this fiber is constant for all points $e$ in the orbit product $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$. The dimension is given by the formula:\n$$ \\dim M_e = (n+1)^2 - \\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y]) $$\nwhere $Y$ is any matrix in $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ such that $\\pi(Y) \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$. The constancy of $\\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y])$ is a consequence of the equivariance of the moment map.\n\n\\textbf{Part 3:} The relevance condition is an equality: $\\dim M_e = \\frac{1}{2} \\dim M - \\frac{1}{2} (\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$. This simplifies to the key equation:\n$$ \\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y]) = \\frac{1}{2}(\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu) $$\nTo find the relevant pairs, a case analysis is performed.\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Case $\\lambda=(1^n)$:} We choose $Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ such that $\\pi(Y)=0$. This forces $Y$ to be of the form $Y = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & u \\\\ v^T & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$. A rigorous analysis of the nilpotency condition ($v^T u = 0$) and the Jordan type of $Y$ based on the ranks of $u$ and $v$ yields a complete classification, resulting in the three \"Zero\" families.\n    \\item \\textbf{Case $\\lambda=(n)$:} The interlacing condition restricts $\\mu$ to be either $(n+1)$ or $(n,1)$. For each of these two possibilities, we construct a specific matrix $Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ with $\\pi(Y)$ being a regular element and verify the relevance condition. This provides a complete classification for this case.\n    \\item \\textbf{Complete analysis for $n=3$:} We analyze all partitions $\\lambda$ of 3. The cases $\\lambda=(1^3)$ and $\\lambda=(3)$ are covered by the general analysis. For the remaining partition $\\lambda=(2,1)$, we test all possible interlacing partitions $\\mu \\in \\{(3,1), (2,2), (2,1,1)\\}$. This involves constructing an appropriate matrix $Y$ for each case, computing the dimension of the projected tangent space $\\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y])$, and verifying the relevance condition. This corrected analysis shows that all three possible pairs are relevant.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{2. Detailed Solution}\n\nLet $\\pi: \\mathfrak{g}_2 \\to \\mathfrak{g}_1$ be the projection onto the top-left $n \\times n$ block. We identify $\\mathfrak{g}_k^*$ with $\\mathfrak{g}_k$ via the trace form $\\langle A, B \\rangle = \\mathrm{Tr}(AB)$. The moment map is $\\mu(g, Y) = (\\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)Y), -Y)$ for $(g, Y) \\in \\mathrm{GL}_{n+1} \\times \\mathfrak{gl}_{n+1}$.\n\n\\subsubsection*{Part 1: Condition on partitions}\n\nThe image of the moment map is $\\mu(M) = \\{ (\\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)Y), -Y) \\mid g \\in G_2, Y \\in \\mathfrak{g}_2 \\}$. The condition $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mu(M)$ implies that for any $X \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$ and $Z \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$, there exists $(g, Y) \\in M$ such that $\\mu(g, Y) = (X, Z)$. This requires $Y = -Z$. Since $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ is a cone, $-Z \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$. The condition becomes that for any $X \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$ and any $Y' \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$, there exists $g \\in G_2$ such that $X = \\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)Y')$.\nThe set of all possible projections from matrices in $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ is $S_\\mu = \\{ \\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)Y) \\mid g \\in G_2, Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\}$. Since $\\mathrm{Ad}(G_2)$ acts transitively on $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu$, this set is simply $S_\\mu = \\{\\pi(Y') \\mid Y' \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu\\}$.\nThe condition is therefore $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subseteq S_\\mu$. Since $S_\\mu$ is a $G_1$-invariant constructible set, this is equivalent to requiring that the intersection is non-empty, i.e., there exists some $Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ such that $\\pi(Y) \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$.\nThis is a known result: a nilpotent matrix $X \\in \\mathfrak{gl}_n$ is a principal submatrix of a nilpotent matrix $Y \\in \\mathfrak{gl}_{n+1}$ if and only if their partitions $\\lambda, \\mu$ satisfy the interlacing condition $\\lambda \\prec \\mu$, which means $\\lambda$ is obtained from $\\mu$ by decreasing one part by 1.\n\n\\subsubsection*{Part 2: Dimension of the fiber}\n\nLet $e = (e_\\lambda, e_\\mu) \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$. The fiber is $M_e = \\mu^{-1}(e) = \\{ (g, Y) \\in M \\mid \\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)Y) = e_\\lambda, -Y=e_\\mu \\}$. This is isomorphic to the variety $S_e = \\{ g \\in \\mathrm{GL}_{n+1} \\mid \\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)(-e_\\mu)) = e_\\lambda \\}$. Since $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$ is a cone, this is equivalent to studying the fiber $S'_e = \\{ g \\in \\mathrm{GL}_{n+1} \\mid \\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)e_\\mu) = -e_\\lambda \\}$.\n\nThe moment map $\\mu$ is $G$-equivariant, where $G=G_1 \\times G_2$ acts on $M$ by $(h_1, h_2) \\cdot (g, Y) = (h_1 g h_2^{-1}, \\mathrm{Ad}(h_2)Y)$ and on $\\mathfrak{g}_1 \\times \\mathfrak{g}_2$ by $(h_1, h_2) \\cdot (X, Z) = (\\mathrm{Ad}(h_1)X, \\mathrm{Ad}(h_2)Z)$. The fibers over any two points in the same $G$-orbit have the same dimension. Thus, $\\dim M_e$ is constant for all $e \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$.\n\nConsider the map $\\phi: \\mathrm{GL}_{n+1} \\to \\mathfrak{g}_1$ defined by $\\phi(g) = \\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)e_\\mu)$. The dimension of the fiber $\\phi^{-1}(-e_\\lambda)$ at a generic point is $\\dim \\mathrm{GL}_{n+1} - \\mathrm{rank}(d_g\\phi)$. The differential at $g$ is $d_g\\phi(A) = \\pi([\\mathrm{Ad}(g)A, \\mathrm{Ad}(g)e_\\mu])$ for $A \\in \\mathfrak{g}_2$. Let $Y = \\mathrm{Ad}(g)e_\\mu$. Then $Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ and $\\pi(Y) = -e_\\lambda \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$. The image of the differential is $\\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y])$.\nThe dimension of the fiber is $\\dim M_e = (n+1)^2 - \\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y])$.\nSince $\\dim M_e$ depends only on the orbits, the quantity $\\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y])$ must be constant for any $Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ such that $\\pi(Y) \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$. This allows us to compute this dimension for any convenient choice of such a $Y$.\n\n\\subsubsection*{Part 3: Relevant orbits}\n\nAn orbit pair is relevant if $\\dim M_e = \\frac{1}{2} \\dim M - \\frac{1}{2} (\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$.\nWith $\\dim M = 2(n+1)^2$, this becomes $(n+1)^2 - \\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y]) = (n+1)^2 - \\frac{1}{2}(\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$.\nThe condition for relevance is:\n$$ \\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y]) = \\frac{1}{2}(\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu) $$\nWe use the dimension formula $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\nu = |\\nu|^2 - \\sum_i (\\nu_i^t)^2$, where $\\nu^t$ is the transpose partition.\n\n\\textbf{Case I: $\\lambda = (1^n)$ (zero orbit)}\nHere $e_\\lambda=0$, so $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda = 0$. We choose $Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ with $\\pi(Y)=0$. Such a $Y$ has the form $Y = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & u \\\\ v^T & c \\end{pmatrix}$. Nilpotency implies $\\mathrm{Tr}(Y)=c=0$ and $\\mathrm{Tr}(Y^2)=2v^Tu=0$.\nFor $A = \\begin{pmatrix} A_{11} & A_{12} \\\\ A_{21} & A_{22} \\end{pmatrix} \\in \\mathfrak{g}_2$, $\\pi([A,Y]) = A_{12}v^T - uA_{21}$. The image space is $S_{u,v} = \\{y v^T - u x^T \\mid y \\in \\mathbb{C}^n, x \\in \\mathbb{C}^{n \\times 1}\\}$. The relevance condition is $\\dim S_{u,v} = \\frac{1}{2}\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item If $u=v=0$, then $Y=0$, so $\\mu=(1^{n+1})$. $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu=0$. $\\dim S_{0,0}=0$. The condition $0=0$ holds. Pair: $(\\lambda, \\mu) = ((1^n), (1^{n+1}))$.\n    \\item If one of $u,v$ is non-zero (say $u \\neq 0, v=0$), then $v^Tu=0$ holds. $Y$ is nilpotent of rank 1, so $\\mu=(2, 1^{n-1})$. $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu = 2n$. The space $S_{u,0}$ consists of matrices whose columns are multiples of $u$, so $\\dim S_{u,0}=n$. The condition $n=\\frac{1}{2}(2n)$ holds. Pair: $(\\lambda, \\mu) = ((1^n), (2, 1^{n-1}))$.\n    \\item If $u,v$ are both non-zero and $v^Tu=0$. $Y$ has rank 2. $Y^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} uv^T & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$. Since $\\mathrm{rank}(uv^T)=1$, $\\mathrm{rank}(Y^2)=1$. $Y^3=0$. The number of Jordan blocks is $\\dim\\ker(Y) = n+1-2=n-1$. The number of blocks of size $\\ge 2$ is $\\dim\\ker(Y^2)-\\dim\\ker(Y) = n-(n-1)=1$. The number of blocks of size $\\ge 3$ is $\\dim\\ker(Y^3)-\\dim\\ker(Y^2) = (n+1)-n=1$. This implies one block of size 3 and $n-2$ blocks of size 1. So $\\mu=(3, 1^{n-2})$ (for $n \\ge 2$).\n    $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_{(3,1^{n-2})} = 4n-2$.\n    The space $S_{u,v}$ is the sum of two spaces: matrices with columns in $\\mathrm{span}(u)$ and matrices with rows in $\\mathrm{span}(v^T)$. Each has dimension $n$. Their intersection is $\\mathrm{span}(uv^T)$, which has dimension 1. So $\\dim S_{u,v} = n+n-1=2n-1$.\n    The relevance condition is $2n-1 = \\frac{1}{2}(4n-2)$, which holds for $n \\ge 2$. Pair: $(\\lambda, \\mu) = ((1^n), (3, 1^{n-2}))$.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\textbf{Case II: $\\lambda = (n)$ (regular orbit)}\n$\\dim \\mathcal{O}_{(n)} = n^2-n$. The condition $\\lambda \\prec \\mu$ implies $\\mu=(n+1)$ or $\\mu=(n,1)$.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Pair $((n), (n+1))$:} $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_{(n+1)} = n^2+n$. RHS of relevance condition: $\\frac{1}{2}(n^2-n + n^2+n) = n^2$.\n    Let $J_n$ be the standard regular nilpotent Jordan block. Let $Y = \\begin{pmatrix} J_n & e_n \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$. $Y$ is a single Jordan block of size $n+1$, so $Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_{(n+1)}$. We have $\\pi(Y)=J_n \\in \\mathcal{O}_{(n)}$.\n    LHS: $\\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y]) = \\dim([\\mathfrak{g}_1, J_n] + S_{e_n, 0})$. The intersection is $\\{0\\}$.\n    LHS = $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_{(n)} + n = (n^2-n)+n = n^2$. LHS=RHS, so the pair is relevant.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Pair $((n), (n,1))$:} $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_{(n,1)} = n^2+n-2$. RHS: $\\frac{1}{2}(n^2-n + n^2+n-2) = n^2-1$.\n    For $n \\ge 2$, let $Y = \\begin{pmatrix} J_n & e_{n-1} \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$. This matrix has Jordan type $(n,1)$.\n    LHS: $\\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y]) = \\dim([\\mathfrak{g}_1, J_n] + S_{e_{n-1}, 0})$. The intersection has dimension 1.\n    LHS = $(n^2-n)+n-1 = n^2-1$. LHS=RHS, so the pair is relevant for $n \\ge 2$. For $n=1$, this is the Zero-Zero case.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\textbf{Case III: Complete analysis for $n=3$}\nThe partitions of $n=3$ are $(1^3), (3), (2,1)$. The first two are covered above. We analyze $\\lambda=(2,1)$.\nLet $X_{21} = E_{21}$ and $X_{12} = E_{12}$ be representatives of $\\mathcal{O}_{(2,1)}$. $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_{(2,1)} = 4$.\nThe partitions $\\mu$ of $4$ with $(2,1) \\prec \\mu$ are $(3,1), (2,2), (2,1,1)$.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Pair $((2,1), (3,1))$:} $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_{(3,1)} = 10$. RHS: $\\frac{1}{2}(4+10)=7$.\n    Let $Y = E_{21}+E_{42} \\in \\mathfrak{gl}_4$. The Jordan chains are $e_1 \\to e_2 \\to e_4 \\to 0$ and $e_3 \\to 0$, so $Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_{(3,1)}$. We have $\\pi(Y)=E_{21} \\in \\mathcal{O}_{(2,1)}$.\n    $\\pi([A,Y]) = A_{11}E_{21} - E_{21}A_{11} + A_{12}e_2^T$.\n    LHS: $\\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y]) = \\dim([\\mathfrak{g}_1, E_{21}] + \\{c e_2^T \\mid c \\in \\mathbb{C}^3\\})$.\n    The intersection of these spaces is $\\{0\\}$.\n    LHS = $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_{(2,1)} + 3 = 4+3=7$. LHS=RHS, so the pair is relevant.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Pair $((2,1), (2,2))$:} $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_{(2,2)} = 8$. RHS: $\\frac{1}{2}(4+8)=6$.\n    Let $Y = \\begin{pmatrix} X_{12} & 0 \\\\ e_3^T & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$. $Y^2=0$ and $\\mathrm{rank}(Y)=2$, so $Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_{(2,2)}$.\n    LHS: $\\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y]) = \\dim([\\mathfrak{g}_1, X_{12}] + S_{0, e_3})$.\n    The intersection is $\\{y e_3^T \\mid y \\perp \\{C^T e_3 \\mid C \\in C(X_{12})\\}\\}$.\n    The centralizer $C(X_{12})$ in $\\mathfrak{gl}_3$ is the set of matrices $C = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b & c \\\\ 0 & a & 0 \\\\ 0 & d & e \\end{pmatrix}$.\n    The space $\\{C^T e_3\\}$ is $\\mathrm{span}(e_2, e_3)$. Its orthogonal complement is $\\mathrm{span}(e_1)$.\n    The intersection is $\\mathrm{span}(e_1 e_3^T)$, which has dimension 1.\n    LHS = $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_{(2,1)} + \\dim S_{0,e_3} - 1 = 4+3-1=6$. LHS=RHS, so the pair is relevant.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Pair $((2,1), (2,1,1))$:} $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_{(2,1,1)} = 6$. RHS: $\\frac{1}{2}(4+6)=5$.\n    Let $Y = \\begin{pmatrix} X_{12} & e_1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$. $Y^2=0$ and $\\mathrm{rank}(Y)=1$, so $Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_{(2,1,1)}$.\n    LHS: $\\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y]) = \\dim([\\mathfrak{g}_1, X_{12}] + S_{e_1, 0})$.\n    The intersection is $\\{e_1 c^T \\mid c \\perp \\{C e_1 \\mid C \\in C(X_{12})\\}\\}$.\n    The space $\\{C e_1\\}$ is $\\mathrm{span}(e_1)$. Its orthogonal complement is $\\mathrm{span}(e_2, e_3)$.\n    The intersection is $\\mathrm{span}(e_1 e_2^T, e_1 e_3^T)$, which has dimension 2.\n    LHS = $4+3-2=5$. LHS=RHS, so the pair is relevant.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\section*{Verification Report}\n\n\\subsection*{Summary}\n\n\\textbf{Final Verdict:} The solution is largely correct and demonstrates a high level of understanding, but contains a minor Justification Gap in its statement of a known result.\n\n\\textbf{List of Findings:}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Location:} \"This is a known result: a nilpotent matrix $X \\in \\mathfrak{gl}_n$ is a principal submatrix of a nilpotent matrix $Y \\in \\mathfrak{gl}_{n+1}$ if and only if their partitions $\\lambda, \\mu$ satisfy the interlacing condition $\\lambda \\prec \\mu$, which means $\\lambda$ is obtained from $\\mu$ by decreasing one part by 1.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Issue:} Justification Gap - The description of the condition on the partitions $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ is imprecise. The correct condition, known as the interlacing condition, is that the Young diagram of $\\lambda$ can be obtained from the Young diagram of $\\mu$ by removing a single box. The solution's description, \"decreasing one part by 1,\" is not always equivalent to this. For a partition $\\mu = (\\mu_1, \\dots, \\mu_k)$, decreasing a part $\\mu_i$ by 1 only results in a valid partition if $\\mu_i > \\mu_{i+1}$ (or if $i=k$). The solution's phrasing does not account for cases with equal adjacent parts.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{Detailed Verification Log}\n\n\\textbf{Initial Setup}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quoted Text:} \"Let $\\pi: \\mathfrak{g}_2 \\to \\mathfrak{g}_1$ be the projection onto the top-left $n \\times n$ block. We identify $\\mathfrak{g}_k^*$ with $\\mathfrak{g}_k$ via the trace form $\\langle A, B \\rangle = \\mathrm{Tr}(AB)$. The moment map is $\\mu(g, Y) = (\\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)Y), -Y)$ for $(g, Y) \\in \\mathrm{GL}_{n+1} \\times \\mathfrak{gl}_{n+1}$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The identification of the dual Lie algebras with the Lie algebras themselves via the trace form is standard. The derivation of the moment map formula from the definitions provided in the problem statement is correct.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Part 1: Condition on partitions}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quoted Text:} \"The image of the moment map is $\\mu(M) = \\{ (\\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)Y), -Y) \\mid g \\in G_2, Y \\in \\mathfrak{g}_2 \\}$. The condition $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mu(M)$ implies that for any $X \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$ and $Z \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$, there exists $(g, Y) \\in M$ such that $\\mu(g, Y) = (X, Z)$. This requires $Y = -Z$. Since $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ is a cone, $-Z \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$. The condition becomes that for any $X \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$ and any $Y' \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$, there exists $g \\in G_2$ such that $X = \\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)Y')$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The reasoning is logical and follows directly from the definitions. The fact that nilpotent orbits in $\\mathfrak{gl}_k(\\mathbb{C})$ are cones (invariant under multiplication by any complex scalar) is used correctly, although only invariance under multiplication by $-1$ is needed, which is true since any nilpotent matrix $N$ is conjugate to $-N$ over $\\mathbb{C}$.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quoted Text:} \"The set of all possible projections from matrices in $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ is $S_\\mu = \\{ \\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)Y) \\mid g \\in G_2, Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\}$. Since $\\mathrm{Ad}(G_2)$ acts transitivity on $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu$, this set is simply $S_\\mu = \\{\\pi(Y') \\mid Y' \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu\\}$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The argument correctly uses the transitivity of the adjoint action on nilpotent orbits to simplify the description of the set $S_\\mu$.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quoted Text:} \"The condition is therefore $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subseteq S_\\mu$. Since $S_\\mu$ is a $G_1$-invariant constructible set, this is equivalent to requiring that the intersection is non-empty, i.e., there exists some $Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ such that $\\pi(Y) \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The argument that $S_\\mu$ is $G_1$-invariant is sound. The equivalence $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subseteq S_\\mu \\iff \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\cap S_\\mu \\neq \\emptyset$ is a standard result for orbits under a group action, given that $S_\\mu$ is invariant under that group's action.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quoted Text:} \"This is a known result: a nilpotent matrix $X \\in \\mathfrak{gl}_n$ is a principal submatrix of a nilpotent matrix $Y \\in \\mathfrak{gl}_{n+1}$ if and only if their partitions $\\lambda, \\mu$ satisfy the interlacing condition $\\lambda \\prec \\mu$, which means $\\lambda$ is obtained from $\\mu$ by decreasing one part by 1.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Justification Gap. The solution correctly identifies that the problem is equivalent to a known result about the Jordan types of a matrix and its principal submatrix. However, the description of the condition on the partitions is imprecise. The correct condition is that the Young diagram of $\\lambda$ is obtained from the Young diagram of $\\mu$ by removing one box. While this is often equivalent to \"decreasing one part by 1,\" it is not a fully accurate statement. For example, if a partition $\\mu$ has equal parts, say $\\mu_i = \\mu_{i+1}$, one cannot decrease $\\mu_i$ by 1 to obtain a valid partition that interlaces $\\mu$. This imprecision does not affect the rest of the solution, as all pairs of partitions considered do satisfy the correct condition. We will assume this step is correct for the remainder of the verification.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Part 2: Dimension of the fiber}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quoted Text:} \"Let $e = (e_\\lambda, e_\\mu) \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$. The fiber is $M_e = \\mu^{-1}(e) = \\{ (g, Y) \\in M \\mid \\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)Y) = e_\\lambda, -Y=e_\\mu \\}$. This is isomorphic to the variety $S_e = \\{ g \\in \\mathrm{GL}_{n+1} \\mid \\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)(-e_\\mu)) = e_\\lambda \\}$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The description of the fiber and its isomorphism to $S_e$ are correct.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quoted Text:} \"The moment map $\\mu$ is $G$-equivariant... The fibers over any two points in the same $G$-orbit have the same dimension. Thus, $\\dim M_e$ is constant for all $e \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The statement of $G$-equivariance is correct, and the conclusion that the fiber dimension is constant over the orbit $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ is a standard consequence.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quoted Text:} \"Consider the map $\\phi: \\mathrm{GL}_{n+1} \\to \\mathfrak{g}_1$ defined by $\\phi(g) = \\pi(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)e_\\mu)$. ... The differential at $g$ is $d_g\\phi(A) = \\pi([\\mathrm{Ad}(g)A, \\mathrm{Ad}(g)e_\\mu])$ for $A \\in \\mathfrak{g}_2$. Let $Y = \\mathrm{Ad}(g)e_\\mu$. ... The image of the differential is $\\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y])$. The dimension of the fiber is $\\dim M_e = (n+1)^2 - \\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y])$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The calculation of the differential of the map $\\phi$ is correct (interpreting $A$ as an element of the Lie algebra corresponding to a tangent vector at $g$). The subsequent formula for the dimension of the fiber using the rank-nullity theorem (for vector spaces) or the fiber dimension theorem (for varieties) is correct.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quoted Text:} \"Since $\\dim M_e$ depends only on the orbits, the quantity $\\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y])$ must be constant for any $Y \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ such that $\\pi(Y) \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. This is a sound deduction from the previous steps, allowing the computation to be performed with a convenient choice of $Y$.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Part 3: Relevant orbits}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quoted Text:} \"The condition for relevance is: $ \\dim \\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y]) = \\frac{1}{2}(\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu) $ We use the dimension formula $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\nu = |\\nu|^2 - \\sum_i (\\nu_i^t)^2$, where $\\nu^t$ is the transpose partition.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The relevance condition is correctly transcribed from the problem statement and combined with the result from Part 2. The dimension formula for nilpotent orbits is standard.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Case I: $\\lambda = (1^n)$ (zero orbit)}\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The analysis for this case is flawless. The characterization of nilpotent matrices $Y$ with $\\pi(Y)=0$ is correct. The three sub-cases based on the ranks of vectors $u,v$ are correctly identified with partitions $\\mu=(1^{n+1})$, $(2, 1^{n-1})$, and $(3, 1^{n-2})$. The calculation of the dimension of the image of the differential, $\\dim S_{u,v}$, is correct in all sub-cases. The final check against the relevance condition is also correct.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Case II: $\\lambda = (n)$ (regular orbit)}\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The analysis for this case is also flawless. The partitions $\\mu$ satisfying $\\lambda \\prec \\mu$ are correctly identified as $(n+1)$ and $(n,1)$. For each pair, a suitable representative matrix $Y$ is chosen. The calculation of the dimension of the image space $\\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y])$ is performed correctly by analyzing the sum of two subspaces and the dimension of their intersection. The final check is correct.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Case III: Complete analysis for $n=3$, $\\lambda=(2,1)$}\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. This case study provides further evidence of the method's validity. The partitions $\\mu$ of 4 such that $(2,1) \\prec \\mu$ are correctly identified. For each of the three resulting pairs, the calculations are executed meticulously.\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item The choice of representative matrices $Y$ is appropriate.\n        \\item The Jordan type of each $Y$ is correctly determined.\n        \\item The computation of the dimension of the image space $\\pi([\\mathfrak{g}_2, Y])$ involves calculating the dimension of an intersection, which relies on correctly identifying the centralizer of a nilpotent matrix. These calculations are performed correctly.\n        \\item The final verification of the relevance condition is correct in all three sub-cases.\n    \\end{itemize}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\end{document}"
}